#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084143} :activity/id #uuid "692fe7ac-6eba-59e0-91c1-ec7dd4e4d67e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050455 :topic/id #uuid "4a511bf0-3faa-5067-bc10-c9e1989452c8" :topic/math-academy-id 724} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084458 :tutorial/content "The **fundamental theorem of algebra** tells us that any quadratic polynomial with real coefficients has precisely two roots. \n\nFurthermore, any such polynomial can be factored as\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = a(x-x_1)(x-x_2),\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the roots of the polynomial.\n\nWhen counting the roots of a quadratic polynomial, we need to bear in mind the following important points:\n\n- If a root has multiplicity $2,$ we count that root twice.\n\n- We also include any imaginary or complex roots.\n\nAlso, remember that in the case of imaginary or complex roots, the roots always come in complex conjugate pairs.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- Let $f(x) = 2x^2 +x -1.$ This polynomial has two distinct real roots, $x = \\dfrac12$ and $x = -1.$ Therefore, it can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-(-1)) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $g(x) = 9x^2-6x+1.$ This polynomial has a single repeated root, $x = \\dfrac13.$ Therefore, it can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =9(x-\\frac{1}{3})(x-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =9{(x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $h(x) = x^2+4.$ This polynomial has two purely imaginary roots, $x = \\pm 2\\textrm i.$ Therefore, it can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $k(x) = x^2-4x+5.$ This polynomial has two complex roots, $x = 2\\pm \\textrm i.$ Therefore, it can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =(x-(2+\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x-2-\\text{i})(x-2+\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8a2af100-5906-5681-b260-d7d94f51dc8d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3739 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a27452eb-dcf8-59d9-baa4-2ffd3a0418ec" :step/math-academy-id 20996 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083237 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083037 :question/id #uuid "65baa4aa-0b90-52d0-91a2-93377e13af18" :question/math-academy-id "e-5788"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0262c8-c029-5a72-846b-1b4f4f3306ba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329787}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given a Double Root and a Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "7cdad979-0b4f-5e49-9162-759975e40f28" :step/math-academy-id 20997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084145}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083238 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083038 :question/id #uuid "98750271-1c2d-5b5f-851c-24963da1e6bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5789"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70c149d8-3739-564c-97c5-6636860ad0c4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050384}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329792}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given Two Imaginary or Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "e12cd4eb-71d9-5a39-93d8-00acad4b3943" :step/math-academy-id 20999 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084146}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083039 :question/id #uuid "0af567b3-a239-5149-b022-d71693c63661" :question/math-academy-id "e-5791"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ee21c78-209f-564b-a005-86f58699a3be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049695}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329794}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quadratic Polynomial With Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "8cdc7989-b37b-58d9-ba73-aada903f432a" :step/math-academy-id 21001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083248 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083044 :question/id #uuid "a9e7aedc-1b2b-5fb7-b8fb-1f9ef7c0643a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5887"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2478305-485c-53a6-9d6c-a72e0764dd4e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329785}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given Two Real Roots and a Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "97173e88-ddbb-59ee-bd62-597dc3ad2844" :step/math-academy-id 21288 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084144}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084486 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we have considered the fundamental theorem of algebra applied to quadratic polynomials. As it turns out, we can apply the fundamental theorem of algebra to *any* polynomial with real coefficients.\n\nConsider a general polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $n$ with real coefficients, given by\n\n$$\np(x) = a_nx^n+\\ldots+a_1x+a_0, \\qquad a_n\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem of algebra tells us that $p(x)$ will always have precisely $n$ roots. \n\nFurthermore, $p(x)$ can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\np(x) = a_n(x-x_1)(x-x_2)(x-x_3)\\cdots (x-x_{n})\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1, x_2, x_3,\\ldots, x_n$ are the roots of the polynomial.\n\nAs with the quadratic case, we need to bear the following important points in mind when counting the roots of a polynomial:\n\n- If a root has multiplicity $m,$ we count that root $m$ times.\n\n- We also include any imaginary or complex roots.\n\nAs with the quadratic case, imaginary and complex roots always come in complex conjugate pairs.\n\nIn future lessons, we will learn more about the fundamental theorem of algebra applied to higher-degree polynomials." :tutorial/id #uuid "8c472d9a-6048-51aa-8381-1c446607a0e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5598 :tutorial/title "General Statement of the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra"} :step/id #uuid "3c4ad85e-6dc8-5d9b-a6ef-50ade8a0f2cd" :step/math-academy-id 34510}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra for Quadratic Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083419}]}
